<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Tickets' cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because other
    sections (Users, Shifts, Budget, Gate, Scanner, MailerLite, Teams, Surveys, Agent) read
    a human's holdings or the order projection through ITicketServiceRead, and Notifications
    reads ITicketSync. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Tickets would make each of them
    reference the section itself and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    Tickets ships both a .Contracts leaf and a Contracts/ folder: the leaf carries what
    cross-section consumers need, the folder inside Humans.Tickets carries public surface that
    is ASP.NET plumbing (TicketStubViewComponent) or the vendor port. "The leaf must stay
    framework-free" is not the reason: FrameworkReference flows transitively through
    ProjectReference, so this leaf resolves Microsoft.AspNetCore.App and could name a
    ViewComponent today. Keeping the view component out is a judgement about what
    cross-section consumers should have to see, not a compiler constraint, and nothing
    enforces it here (Peter, 2026-08-15).

    Three of the members here exist so that Tickets is the application's only door to
    ticketing: ITicketDiscountCodes and ITicketVendorMirror move Campaigns' and Gate's
    (GateVendorCheckInJob, Humans.Gate/Jobs/) calls off the vendor port
    (ITicketVendorService), which is injected only inside Humans.Tickets — its own
    Health/TicketVendorHealthCheck included.
    The port itself is deliberately NOT here: it sits in Humans.Tickets/Contracts/,
    because no Base consumer names it and
    Humans.TicketTailor references Humans.Tickets directly to implement it.
    TicketDiscountCodeRequest / TicketDiscountKind are the
    section's own boundary vocabulary, mapped to the port's DiscountCodeSpec /
    DiscountType inside the section (Finance's CreditorLedgerLine shape): the leaf must
    never name the port's types, or the 2027 vendor swap reaches the callers.

    Everything with no consumer outside the section stays internal: the whole
    TicketDashboardDtos surface (24 types), the transfer wizard and admin DTOs, the
    contact-import and onsite-roster services, the repositories and the entities.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, SurfaceBudget, ExternalWrite) and
    NodaTime. Not Humans.Domain: TicketAttendeeStatus and TicketPaymentStatus are named by
    the DTOs here and moved onto the leaf with them (Budget's rule), and nothing else the
    leaf names is Base vocabulary.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Base\Humans.Base.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
